<title>USA: USDA puts 1997/98 cotton exports at 6.3-7.3 mln.</title>
<headline>USDA puts 1997/98 cotton exports at 6.3-7.3 mln.</headline>
<dateline>NEW ORLEANS 1997-01-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Agriculture Department said cotton exports in 1997/98 could climb to between 6.3 million and 7.3 million 480-lb bales, compared to an estimated 6.2 million bales this season.</p>
<p>Stephen MacDonald, an economist with USDA, said the U.S. should be in a position to supply the cotton for an anticipated rise in world consumption, as early projections show an increase in U.S. output.</p>
<p>He said the export figures represent an improvement in the U.S. share of world cotton trade in 1997/98 to 25 percent to 26 percent, from the current 23.5 percent.</p>
<p>He spoke at the Beltwide Cotton Conference in New Orleans.</p>
<p>((New York Commodities 212-859-1640))</p>
</text>
